<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Shah Abdali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1722–<a href="/years/1772">1772</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Ahmad Shah Durrani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Shah Abdali, also known as Ahmad Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a>, was an Afghan commander in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s army who founded the Durrani Empire after Nader's death in 1747. He invaded northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> several times and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-battle-of-panipat">Third Battle of Panipat</a> in 1761. His campaigns weakened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> and influenced the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-abdali.jpg" alt="Ahmad Shah Abdali" width="311" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Ahmad-Shah_Durrani._Mughal_miniature._ca._1757,_Biblioth%C3%A8que_nationale_de_France_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmad Khan was born in the early 1720s into the Abdali tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pashtuns</a>. Sources differ on whether he was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>; modern scholarship generally favours Multan, though Sajjad Nejatie argues that most contemporary sources, including the Tarikh-i Ahmad Shahi, say Herat. His father Zaman Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Herat, died in 1721, and he was raised with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfiqar-khan">Zulfiqar Khan</a> in Shindand and Farah. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a> defeated Zulfiqar Khan in 1731 to 1732, both brothers fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> and were held as political prisoners by the Hotak king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussain-hotak">Hussain Hotak</a>. Freed after Nader Shah conquered Kandahar, Ahmad accompanied him on his invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1738 to 1739 and enlisted in the Afsharid army in 1742.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nader-shah">Nader Shah</a>'s assassination in 1747 Ahmad Shah went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a> with his regiments, made himself leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> tribesmen and killed his uncle 'Abd al-Ghani Khan, the governor. On the way he seized a convoy carrying the annual tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, and his army grew to over 18,000. A legend that a jirga of tribal leaders chose him unanimously, placing wheat or barley on his turban, has no contemporary evidence. His tribe changed its name from Abdali to Durrani. In autumn 1747 he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> from Nasir Khan, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshawar">Peshawar</a>.</p><p>He began a series of invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1748 that lasted 24 years. He annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> after his third invasion, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1757 in his fourth and destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> armies at the Third Battle of Panipat in his fifth, the largest battle of the 18th century. He fought over fifteen major campaigns, nine centred in India, three in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khorasan">Khorasan</a> and three in Afghan Turkestan. Historians record that his campaigns left northern India in chaos, with massacres, pillaging and mass enslavement by Afghan soldiers. His government copied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a>, and he exempted his own tribe from taxation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Illness, perhaps a malignant wound on the nose or a facial cancer, left him unable to speak by summer 1772. He died at a palace at Maruf, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, on 16 or 23 October 1772, and was buried in Kandahar, where his son Timur Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> built a mausoleum in 1777. He had named Timur as heir over his eldest son Sulaiman Mirza, which led to a short civil war that Timur won without battle. Historians rank him among the foremost generals of eighteenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, and he is often regarded as the founder of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Afghans call him Ahmad Shāh Bābā, Ahmad Shah the Father.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad Shah Durrani” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ahmad Shah Durrani</b>, formerly commander of Nader's Abdali Afghan contingent, was chosen as ruler at Kandahar within months of Nader's death and founded the Durrani Empire that became the basis of modern Afghanistan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Durrani Empire (1747–1823) was built by <b>Ahmad Shah Durrani</b> from the wreck of Nader Shah's Persia into a Pashtun state reaching from Khorasan to the Punjab, victorious over the Marathas at Panipat in 1761 but unable to hold down the Sikhs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Conflict with the Durrani Empire of <b>Ahmad Shah Abdali</b> over the Punjab and northern India ended in the catastrophic defeat at the Third Battle of Panipat in 1761, which inflicted heavy military and human losses and disrupted revenue systems.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>External invasions, by Nader Shah of Persia, who sacked Delhi in 1739 and carried off the Peacock Throne, and later by <b>Ahmad Shah Durrani</b>, further destabilised the empire and depleted its wealth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Punjab in this period was a contested borderland, subject to repeated Afghan invasions under <b>Ahmad Shah Durrani</b> and his successors, and the Misls, though strong in the field, remained chronically disunited.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Through the 18th century Sikhs endured persecution and the invasions of Nader Shah and <b>Ahmad Shah Durrani</b>, who blew up the Harmandir Sahib in 1762, and organised themselves into confederacies, the misls, which gradually took control of the Punjab as Mughal power collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Spread</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kandahar">Kandahar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1754" aria-label="Herat on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6132 920 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahmad Shah Abdali"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6582 1190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6582 1190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.34,62.2&amp;year=1754">What was here in 1754? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
